#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee867b9-645a-512d-9f99-f3aa8226ad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327063} {:answer/id #uuid "4558acbf-a492-5e1c-acde-0aa26d25d7f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327540} {:answer/id #uuid "a58a5f87-8e65-5592-8409-a19f0dfcd0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328364} {:answer/id #uuid "b013ab09-8aad-54e4-9a17-87cdc1aa5d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328460} {:answer/id #uuid "c6636203-3372-5d9c-9639-986cdeaf7e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ee867b9-645a-512d-9f99-f3aa8226ad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327063} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45de7c6-c5ce-5b9d-a7b8-ea5254d09158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96069899-bca3-51de-b64c-6e29072ea13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48062" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9db7f2a491ae3065dbe8434a71d378c4fed8fec16c82e2fd76dcb209c786809.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f05bd8f-eb2f-57d7-ae18-c11817bd78b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327620} {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9a91aa-3ed8-5151-bcb5-e59ccd13d6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328272} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9e8871-1f30-5744-8374-042f56698b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328750} {:answer/id #uuid "d6472410-aa22-5e41-a0c0-09e47c3fb93c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e84077-b88d-5119-bcf7-6c45cbfd0278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e01edc7-98f4-52fe-bf43-cb1e05ae0d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48065" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa15192923f9fd7370818fcb6b05b24b3bb6901dfbf90dd53539d71fd0f8b0bf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01aeaace-3dee-5861-928d-6a23090d00f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345366} {:answer/id #uuid "01c007c5-b958-5f05-bcdb-d16eb849a4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345367} {:answer/id #uuid "02100b32-fbcc-582d-903e-bf23af1e8c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345368} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a6767c-d2f1-526e-934e-fbbf34e09777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347068} {:answer/id #uuid "f40cbba4-ff28-567c-bde7-f1a1ff700f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f40cbba4-ff28-567c-bde7-f1a1ff700f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347512} :answer-field/id #uuid "01de2c48-cbc5-5a34-8784-842c895031e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c2c6cc4-6d0f-5ca4-bd69-989208f699b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-78132" :question/problem "![](images/89/890e7eaf519a65ca19d4a90b604e7d7a8ab7054635296c4b08f000546f23b545.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=-2$\n\nII. $x=1$\n\nIII. $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $-2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of  $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and III only\".\n\n![](images/7c/7c96c401ddae0049859fd5608cb08c6ce9ae8a2e6c3db69d7d4b379b3824094e.png)"}]}
